{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n 'github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues|term tracker|tracker|source|IAO_0000119|hasDbXref \\\"GITHUB|GitHub|issue' src/ontology/cl-edit.owl\"","aggregated_output":"3723:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 cl:has_high_plasma_membrane_amount <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/1481>)\n3731:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 cl:has_low_plasma_membrane_amount <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/1481>)\n3761:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"ReO:mhb\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000001 \"A cultured cell that is freshly isolated from a organismal source, or derives in culture from such a cell prior to the culture being passaged.\")\n3785:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_0000003 <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/2124>)\n3833:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"ISBN:0471245208\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000009 \"Obsolete. Use PO:0000079 from Plant Ontology instead. An elongated cell with approximately wedge-shaped ends, found in the vascular cambium, which gives rise to the elements of the axial system in the secondary vascular tissues.\")\n3885:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso obo:CL_0000015 \"https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/574\")\n4090:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:17289566\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"doi:/10.1038/sj.eye.6700575\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000036 \"A somatic stem cell that is committed to an epithelial fate, possessing the capacity for self-renewal and the ability to differentiate into one or more distinct mature cell types of the epithelial lineage. This cell is crucial for the development, homeostasis, repair, and regeneration of epithelial tissues.\")\n4308:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_0000054 <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/2698>)\n4317:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_0000055 \"https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/2922\")\n4339:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"ISBN:0517223651\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"MESH:D005347\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fibroblast\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000057 \"A connective tissue cell which secretes an extracellular matrix rich in collagen and other macromolecules. Flattened and irregular in outline with branching processes; appear fusiform or spindle-shaped.\")\n4353:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GO_REF:0000034\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"ISBN:0618947256\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000058 \"Skeletogenic cell that is typically non-terminally differentiated, secretes an avascular, GAG rich matrix; is not buried in cartilage tissue matrix, retains the ability to divide, located adjacent to cartilage tissue (including within the perichondrium), and develops from prechondroblast (and thus prechondrogenic) cell.\")\n4411:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GO_REF:0000034\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"MESH:D010006\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000062 \"Skeletogenic cell that secretes osteoid, is capable of producing mineralized (hydroxyapatite) matrix, is located adjacent to or within osteoid tissue, and arises from the transformation of a preosteoblast cell.\")\n4506:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_0000073 <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/1149>)\n4532:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"ISBN:0721662544\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:26106328\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000077 \"A flat, squamous-like epithelial cell of mesodermal origin. It forms the mesothelium, which lines the body's serous cavities including the pleural, peritoneal, and pericardial spaces. This cell plays a crucial role in synthesizing and secreting lubricants, such as glycosaminoglycans and surfactants, which minimize friction between adjacent tissues during movement.\")\n4555:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:add\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000080 \"A cell which moves among different tissues of the body, via blood, lymph, or other medium.\")\n4607:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_0000085 <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/1935>)\n4615:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_0000086 <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/1935>)\n4623:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_0000087 <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/1935>)\n4631:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_0000088 <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/1935>)\n4639:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_0000089 <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/1935>)\n4647:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_0000090 <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/1935>)\n4655:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:add\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GO_REF:0000031\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"ISBN:0517223651\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"MESH:D007728\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:15771589\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:17105582\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000091 \"A tissue-resident macrophage of the reticuloendothelial system found on the luminal surface of the hepatic sinusoids involved in erythrocyte clearance. Markers include F4/80+, CD11b-low, CD68-positive, sialoadhesin-positive, CD163/SRCR-positive. Irregular, with long processes including lamellipodia extending into the sinusoid lumen, have flattened nucleus with cytoplasm containing characteristic invaginations of the plasma membrane (vermiform bodies); lie within the sinusoid lumen attached to the endothelial surface; derived from the bone marrow, form a major part of the body's mononuclear phagocyte system.\")\n4671:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:add\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GO_REF:0000031\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"ISBN:0781735149\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:10428500\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:15055519\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:17380158\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000092 \"A specialized phagocytic cell associated with the absorption and removal of the mineralized matrix of bone tissue, which typically differentiates from monocytes. This cell has the following markers: tartrate-resistant acid phosphatase type 5-positive, PU.1-positive, c-fos-positive, nuclear factor NF-kappa-B p100 subunit-positive, tumor necrosis factor receptor superfamily member 11A-positive and macrophage colony-stimulating factor 1 receptor-positive.\")\n4677:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:CL_0000092 \"Morphology: Highly vesicular; markers: Surface: RANK, cFMS (MCSF receptor); Secreted: cathepsin K and TRAP (tartate resistant acid phosphatase); transcription factors: PU.1, cFOS, MITF, NFkB (p52); role or process: tissue remodelling: bone resorption; lineage: hematopoietic, myeloid.\")\n4735:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:add\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:dsd\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"ISBN:068340007X\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"MESH:D008407\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMCID:PMC1312421\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMCID:PMC2855166\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:15153310\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:16455980\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:19671378\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:212366338\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:9354811\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000097 \"A cell that is found in almost all tissues containing numerous basophilic granules and capable of releasing large amounts of histamine and heparin upon activation. Progenitors leave bone marrow and mature in connective and mucosal tissue. Mature mast cells are found in all tissues, except the bloodstream. Their phenotype is CD117-high, CD123-negative, CD193-positive, CD200R3-positive, and FceRI-high. Stem-cell factor (KIT-ligand; SCF) is the main controlling signal of their survival and development.\")\n4742:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"ISBN:068340007X\") oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:CL_0000097 \"tissue basophil\")\n4868:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_0000111 <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/1069>)\n4905:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"MESH:D042783\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:21275341\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Endothelial_cell\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://sourceforge.net/tracker/?func=detail&atid=440764&aid=3364936&group_id=36855\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000115 \"An endothelial cell comprises the outermost layer or lining of anatomical structures and can be squamous or cuboidal. In mammals, endothelial cell has vimentin filaments and is derived from the mesoderm.\")\n4994:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_0000124 <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/715>)\n5007:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:CL_0000125 \"Not all glial cells develop from glioblasts, with microglia developing from the mesoderm instead. See https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/1571\")\n5083:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:21976958\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:23284695\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:34741068\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:36769303\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000132 \"An hexagonal, flattened, mitochondria-rich endothelial cell that forms a monolayer on the posterior surface of the cornea (the corneal endothelium). Corneal endothelial cells are derived from the neural crest and are responsible for keeping the cornea transparent by maintaining the tissue in a semi-dry state through the action of their ionic pumps and tight junction barrier.\")\n5093:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"ISBN:068340007X\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000133 \"Ectoderm destined to be nervous tissue.\")\n5100:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"FB:ma\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:dsd\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMCID:PMC2613570\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:10102814\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:16923606\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:17986482\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:19960544\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mesenchymal_stem_cell\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"http://www.copewithcytokines.de/cope.cgi?key=mesenchymal%20stem%20cells\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000134 \"A connective tissue cell that normally gives rise to other cells that are organized as three-dimensional masses. In humans, this cell type is CD73-positive, CD90-positive, CD105-positive, CD45-negative, CD34-negative, and MHCII-negative. They may further differentiate into osteoblasts, adipocytes, myocytes, neurons, or chondroblasts in vitro. Originally described as residing in the bone marrow, this cell type is now known to reside in many, if not all, adult organs.\")\n5118:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso obo:CL_0000134 \"https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/474\")\n5138:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"MESH:D017667\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000136 \"A fat-storing cell found mostly in the abdominal cavity and subcutaneous tissue of mammals. Fat is usually stored in the form of triglycerides.\")\n5165:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GO_REF:0000034\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"MESH:D019902\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:32491508\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000138 \"A skeletogenic cell that secretes a specialized, avascular, GAG-rich matrix, is embedded in cartilage tissue matrix, retains the ability to divide, and develops from a chondroblast cell.\")\n5186:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GO_REF:0000034\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000140 \"Skeletogenic cell that secretes dentine matrix, is derived from odontogenic papilla. Embedded in dentine tissue, and is the transformation of a non-terminally differentiated odontoblast cell.\")\n5369:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_0000161 <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/427>)\n5572:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_0000181 <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/720>)\n5829:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_0000212 <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/2271>)\n5836:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"JB:jb\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000213 \"OBSOLETE. A cell within an epithelial cell sheet whose main function is to act as an internal or external covering for a tissue or an organism.\")\n5837:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_0000213 <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/3014>)\n5855:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_0000215 <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/3012>)\n5875:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_0000217 <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/3013>)\n6040:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:add\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GO_REF:0000031\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:16213494\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:1919437\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000235 \"A mononuclear phagocyte present in variety of tissues, typically differentiated from monocytes, capable of phagocytosing a variety of extracellular particulate material, including immune complexes, microorganisms, and dead cells.\")\n6047:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:CL_0000235 \"Morphology: Diameter 30_M-80 _M, abundant cytoplasm, low N/C ratio, eccentric nucleus. Irregular shape with pseudopods, highly adhesive. Contain vacuoles and phagosomes, may contain azurophilic granules; markers: Mouse & Human: CD68, in most cases CD11b. Mouse: in most cases F4/80+; role or process: immune, antigen presentation, & tissue remodelling; lineage: hematopoietic, myeloid.\")\n6121:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_0000243 <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/715>)\n6265:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"ISBN:0471245208\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000264 \"Obsolete. Use PO:0000400 from Plant Ontology instead. A cambial cell on the phloem side of the cambial zone that is the source of one or more cells arising by periclinal divisions and differentiating into phloem elements with or without additional divisions in various planes.\")\n6279:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"ISBN:0471245208\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000266 \"Obsolete. Use PO:0004011 from Plant Ontology instead. A cell constituting an active locus of meristematic activity in a tissue composed of somewhat older, differentiating cells.\")\n6292:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"ISBN:0471245208\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000268 \"Obsolete. Consult Plant Ontology instead. The cell in the phloem tissue concerned with mainly longitudinal conduction of food materials. Classified into sieve cell and sieve tube member.\")\n6304:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"ISBN:047124520\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000270 \"Obsolete. Use PO:0000284 from Plant Ontology instead. An epidermal cell associated with a stoma and at least morphologically distinguishable from the epidermal cells composing the groundmass of the tissue.\")\n6317:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"ISBN:0471245208\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000272 \"OBSOLETE, use term from Plant Ontology. A cell localized in the vascular cambium or phellogen. Its periclinal division can contribute cells either to the outside or to the inside of the axis; in vascular cambium, classified into fusiform initials (source of axial cells of xylem and phloem) and ray initials (source of the ray cells).\")\n6434:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"ISBN:0471245208\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000290 \"Obsolete. Use PO:0000274 from Plant Ontology instead. A fiber of the xylem tissue, Two types are recognized in the secondary xylem: fiber tracheids and libriform fibers.\")\n6456:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_0000293 <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/3010>)\n6588:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:15749908\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:19256306\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:19727116\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:29713660\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:37873034\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000312 \"An epithelial cell of stratified squamous tissues, including skin, oral mucosa (Hiroshima et al., 2011), and esophagus (Whelan et al., 2018), that produces keratin proteins and secretes antimicrobial peptides to form a resilient barrier against environmental damage, dehydration, pathogens, and microbial invasion. It undergoes successive stages of differentiation marked by changes in keratin expression, supporting tissue integrity, wound repair, and contributing to immune defense.\")\n6661:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_0000321 <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/1149>)\n6716:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:CL_0000328 \"see: https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/161\")\n6988:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"ISBN:0471245208\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000364 \"Obsolete. Consult Plant Ontology. A cell that develops from the ray initial and composes all rays (panels of tissue variable in height and width, formed by the ray initials in the vascular cambium and extending radially in the secondary xylem and secondary phloem) in the secondary vascular tissues.\")\n7110:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_0000381 <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/1784>)\n7144:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_0000386 <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/3016>)\n7179:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_0000391 <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/1949>)\n7200:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://doi.org/10.1016/B978-012369493-5.50008-0\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000394 \"A phagocytic hemocyte, responsible for the engulfment of small particles, microbes, and apoptotic tissue debris. It may also secretes antimicrobial peptides and contribute to the production and secretion of proteins of the hemolymph.\")\n7226:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_0000398 <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/1949>)\n7247:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_0000401 <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/1949>)\n7256:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_0000402 <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/2869>)\n7276:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_0000405 <https://github.com/FlyBase/drosophila-anatomy-developmental-ontology/issues/1621>)\n7424:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_0000426 <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/708>)\n7425:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:CL_0000426 \"This is a cell used in tissue engineering and is out of scope for CL.\")\n7468:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"ISBN:0517223651\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reticular_cell\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000432 \"A fibroblast that synthesizes collagen and uses it to produce reticular fibers, thus providing structural support. Reticular cells are found in many organs, including the spleen, lymph nodes and kidneys. Subtypes of reticular cells include epithelial, mesenchymal, and fibroblastic reticular cells. Fibroblastic reticular cells are involved in directing B cells and T cells to specific regions within a tissue, whereas epithelial and mesenchymal reticular cells are associated with certain areas of the brain.\")\n7472:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso obo:CL_0000432 \"https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/1472\")\n7543:SubClassOf(Annotation(obo:IAO_0000233 \"https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/1943\") obo:CL_0000441 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0002162 obo:NCBITaxon_6656))\n7547:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"ISBN:127520252\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000442 \"A cell with extensive dendritic processes found in the B cell areas (primary follicles and germinal centers) of lymphoid tissue. They are unrelated to the dendritic cell associated with T cells. Follicular dendritic cells have Fc receptors and C3b receptors, but unlike other dendritic cells, they do not process or present antigen in a way that allows recognition by T cells. Instead, they hold antigen in the form of immune complexes on their surfaces for long periods and can present antigen to B cells during an immune response.\")\n7606:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"MESH:D002001\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000449 \"A cell from the thermogenic form of adipose tissue found in many species,  particularly in newborns and hibernating mammals, but also in lesser amounts in adults of other mammals including humans. Brown fat is capable of rapid liberation of energy and seems to be important in the maintenance of body temperature immediately after birth and upon waking from hibernation.\")\n7624:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:add\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"ISBN:0781735149\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000451 \"A cell of hematopoietic origin, typically resident in particular tissues, specialized in the uptake, processing, and transport of antigens to lymph nodes for the purpose of stimulating an immune response via T cell activation. These cells are lineage negative (CD3-negative, CD19-negative, CD34-negative, and CD56-negative).\")\n7639:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_0000452 <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/3161>)\n7792:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"ISBN:0471245208\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000472 \"Obsolete. Use PO:0000076 from Plant Ontology instead. OBSOLETE, use term from Plant Ontology. Parenchyma cells containing chloroplasts; a component of leaf mesophyll and other green parenchyma tissue.\")\n7840:SubClassOf(Annotation(oboInOwl:source <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/589>) obo:CL_0000477 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0002162 obo:NCBITaxon_50557))\n7879:# Class: obo:CL_0000484 (connective tissue type mast cell)\n7881:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:add\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:dsd\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:19923473\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:9354811\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000484 \"Mast cell subtype whose granules contain both the serine proteases tryptase and chymase. These cells are primarily found in connective tissue, such as the peritoneal cavity, skin, and intestinal submucosa. Their development is T-cell independent.\")\n7886:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0000484 \"connective tissue type mast cell\")\n7892:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:add\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:dsd\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:19923473\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:9354811\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000485 \"Mast cell subtype that contains only the serine protease trypase in its granules. These cells are primarily found in mucosal tissue, such as intestinal mucosa and alveoli. They depend upon T-cells for development of phenotype.\")\n7999:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"MESH:D017154\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000499 \"A connective tissue cell of an organ found in the loose connective tissue.\")\n8217:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso obo:CL_0000525 \"https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/2100\")\n8317:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"ISBN:0471245208\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000539 \"OBSOLETE: A cell in a tissue that markedly differs in form, size, or contents from other cells in the same tissue.\")\n8323:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"MESH:D009474\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neuron\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000540 \"The basic cellular unit of nervous tissue. Each neuron consists of a body, an axon, and dendrites. Their purpose is to receive, conduct, and transmit impulses in the nervous system.\")\n8430:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_0000548 <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/2124>)\n8666:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:18816864\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000569 \"A mesenchymal cell found in the developing heart and that develops into some part of the heart.  These cells derive from intra- and extra-cardiac sources, including the endocardium, epicardium, neural crest, and second heart field.\")\n8731:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GO_REF:0000031\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"MESH:D009000\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000576 \"Myeloid mononuclear recirculating leukocyte that can act as a precursor of tissue macrophages, osteoclasts and some populations of tissue dendritic cells.\")\n8735:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:CL_0000576 \"Morphology: Mononuclear cell, diameter, 14 to 20 _M, N/C ratio 2:1-1:1. Nucleus may appear in variety of shapes: round, kidney, lobulated, or convoluted. Fine azurophilic granules present; markers: CD11b (shared with other myeloid cells), human: CD14, mouse: F4/80-mid,GR1-low; location: Blood, but can be recruited into tissues; role or process: immune & tissue remodelling; lineage: hematopoietic, myeloid.\")\n8808:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:ana\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:dsd\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GO_REF:0000031\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"MESH:D016676\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000583 \"A tissue-resident macrophage found in the alveoli of the lungs. Ingests small inhaled particles resulting in degradation and presentation of the antigen to immunocompetent cells. Markers include F4/80-positive, CD11b-/low, CD11c-positive, CD68-positive, sialoadhesin-positive, dectin-1-positive, MR-positive, CX3CR1-negative.\")\n9208:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"TAIR:sr\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000627 \"OBSOLETE. A cell involved in transporting nutrients, minerals, water, gases and other chemicals between cells for a variety of purposes including conveying nutrition to other tissues, removing waste products from the tissues, conveying gases for respiration, distributing heat and repelling invasion of foreign substances.\")\n9209:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_0000627 <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/2990>)\n9216:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"TAIR:sr\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000628 \"A cell that can perform photosynthesis, in which carbohydrates are synthesized from carbon dioxide and water, using light as the energy source.\")\n9378:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"ISBN:0412046911\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"ISBN:0517223651\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:31915267\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pituicyte\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000645 \"A glial cell of astrocytic lineage with long processes running parallel to adjacent axons in the proximal infundibulum of the neurohypophysis. These processes form a three-dimensional network among the axons of the hypothalamic neurosecretory cells and are connected by gap junctions which provide for their metabolic coupling. This cell type constitutes most of the nonexcitable tissue in the neurohypophsis; function may include possibly acting as an intermediate in the modulation of oxytocin and vasopressin release. This cell type is highly variable in size and shape and commonly contain lipid droplets and deposits of lipochrome pigment.\")\n9479:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso obo:CL_0000653 \"https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/1460\")\n9556:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_0000663 <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/726>)\n9563:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_0000664 <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/726>)\n9570:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_0000665 <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/726>)\n9577:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"DOI:10.1007/978-3-211-99390-3_133\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000666 \"An endothelial cell that has small pores, or fenestrations, which allow for the efficient exchange of substances between the blood and surrounding tissues.\")\n9591:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"ISBN:0471245208\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000668 \"OBSOLETE: The cell characteristic of an organ, as distinguished from associated connective or supporting tissues.\")\n9996:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_0000717 <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/1364>)\n10063:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_0000727 <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/1930>)\n10070:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_0000728 <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/1930>)\n10077:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_0000729 <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/1930>)\n10141:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:add\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"ISBN:978-0-323-05290-0\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000738 \"An achromatic cell of the myeloid or lymphoid lineages capable of ameboid movement, found in blood or other tissue.\")\n10194:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:35179487\") rdfs:comment obo:CL_0000743 \"Despite historical descriptions as 'terminally differentiated,' hypertrophic chondrocytes survive and transdifferentiate into osteoblasts. Genetic lineage tracing shows that hypertrophic chondrocytes do not simply undergo apoptosis but cross the cartilage–bone boundary, dedifferentiate into skeletal stem/progenitor cells, and then redifferentiate into osteoblasts and osteocytes. Single-cell RNA-seq further indicates that these chondrocyte-derived SSPCs are a major source of trabecular osteoblasts and marrow adipocytes.\")\n10547:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:add\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"ISBN:0702022918\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"ISBN:3540536663\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:1600140\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:16146841\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000777 \"A tissue-resident macrophage of the renal glomerular mesangium involved in the disposal and degradation of filtration residues, presentation of antigen to T cells and in tissue remodeling.\")\n10838:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:add\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"ISBN:0781735149\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000800 \"A gamma-delta T cell that has a mature phenotype. These cells can be found in tissues and circulation where they express unique TCR repertoire depending on their location.\")\n11087:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:add\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:dsd\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GO_REF:0000031\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:11861604\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:20933013\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:21220451\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/B-1_cell\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000819 \"A B cell of distinct lineage and surface marker expression. B-1 B cells are thought to be the primary source of natural IgM immunoglobulin, that is, IgM produced in large quantities without prior antigenic stimulation and generally reactive against various microorganisms, as well as the source of T-independent IgA immunoglobulin in the mucosal areas. These cells are CD43-positive.\")\n11348:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:add\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:9521319\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000840 \"An immature cell of the conventional dendritic cell lineage, characterized by high levels of antigen uptake via endocytosis, macropinocytosis, and phagocytosis, and typically found resident in the tissues. Markers for this cell are CD80-low, CD86-low, and MHC-II-low.\")\n11549:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:add\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:ana\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GO_REF:0000031\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:15771589\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000861 \"A macrophage which develops from an inflammatory monocyte and is recruited into the tissues in response to injury and infection as part of an inflammatory response. Markers include CD11b-positive, CD68-positive, and F4/80-positive.\")\n11570:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:ana\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GO_REF:0000031\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:19132917\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000863 \"An elicited macrophage that is recruited into the tissues in response to injury and infection as part of an inflammatory response, expresses high levels of pro-inflammatory cytokines, ROS and NO, and shows potent microbicidal activity.\")\n11579:# Class: obo:CL_0000864 (tissue-resident macrophage)\n11581:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:ana\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GO_REF:0000031\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:19770654\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000864 \"A macrophage constitutively resident in a particular tissue under non-inflammatory conditions, and capable of phagocytosing a variety of extracellular particulate material, including immune complexes, microorganisms, and dead cells.\")\n11586:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0000864 \"tissue-resident macrophage\")\n11592:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:ana\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GO_REF:0000031\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://orcid.org/0000-0002-2825-0621\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000865 \"A gut-associated lymphoid tissue macrophage found in lamina propria of the gut.\")\n11601:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:ana\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GO_REF:0000031\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:9048205\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000866 \"A tissue-resident macrophage resident found in the thymus, involved in the clearance of apoptotic thymocytes.\")\n11609:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:ana\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GO_REF:0000031\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000867 \"A tissue-resident macrophage found in a secondary lymphoid organ.\")\n11624:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:ana\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GO_REF:0000031\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:16048540\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000869 \"A gut-associated lymphoid tissue macrophage found in tonsils.\")\n11631:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:ana\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GO_REF:0000031\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000870 \"A gut-associated lymphoid tissue macrophage found in the Peyer's patches.\")\n11671:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:add\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GO_REF:0000031\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:16322748\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:18303997\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:20628149\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:20870168\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000875 \"A type of monocyte characterized by low expression of CCR2, low responsiveness to monocyte chemoattractant CCL2/MCP1, low phagocytic activity, and decrease size relative to classical monocytes, but increased co-stimulatory activity. May also play a role in tissue repair.\")\n11697:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GO_REF:0000031\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:16322748\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000878 \"A tissue-resident macrophage found in the central nervous system.\")\n11745:# Class: obo:CL_0000884 (mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue macrophage)\n11747:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:ana\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GO_REF:0000031\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:19906191\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000884 \"A tissue-resident macrophage found in the mucosa associated lymphoid tissue.\")\n11748:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0000884 \"mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue macrophage\")\n11752:# Class: obo:CL_0000885 (gut-associated lymphoid tissue macrophage)\n11754:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:ana\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GO_REF:0000031\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:19906191\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000885 \"A mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue macrophage found in the mucosa-associated lymphoid tissues of the gut.\")\n11755:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0000885 \"gut-associated lymphoid tissue macrophage\")\n11759:# Class: obo:CL_0000886 (nasal and broncial associated lymphoid tissue macrophage)\n11761:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:ana\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GO_REF:0000031\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:16048540\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000886 \"A mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue macrophage found in the nasal and bronchial mucosa-associated lymphoid tissues.\")\n11762:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0000886 \"nasal and broncial associated lymphoid tissue macrophage\")\n11783:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:CL_0000889 \"Markers: Mouse: CD11b+GR1+CD31+; Human: CD34+ CD33+CD15-CD13+. (According to some reports in humans these cells are iNOS+ARG1+IL13+IFNg+); location: In cancerous tissue; in the blood and lymphoid organs in sepsis.\")\n11790:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:ana\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GO_REF:0000031\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:20510870\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000890 \"An elicited macrophage characterized by low production of pro-inflammatory and Th1 polarizing cytokines and high expression of arginase-1, and associated with tissue remodelling.\")\n11793:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:CL_0000890 \"Role or process: tissue remodeling.\")\n12067:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:PAM-ADD\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"ISBN:0877799148\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:18395547\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000911 \"A differentiated T cell with ability to traffic to peripheral tissues and is capable of mounting a specific immune response.\")\n12447:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:add\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:pam\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GO_REF:0000031\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:19416870\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:33352411\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0000940 \"An alpha-beta T cell that is found in the lamina propria of mucosal tissues and is restricted by the MR-1 molecule. This cell develops from double-positive, alpha-beta thymocyte.\")\n13387:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:add\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GO_REF:0000034\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0001035 \"A connective tissue cell found in bone.\")\n13395:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"VSAO:curator\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0001039 \"Osteoblast that is terminally differentiated, located adjacent to acellular or cellular bone tissue within periosteum, and is capable of mineralizing the matrix.\")\n13402:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"VSAO:curator\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0001040 \"Osteoblast that is non-terminally differentiated and located in cellular bone tissue or under the periosteum in acellular bone.\")\n13651:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:CL_0001061 \"https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/448\")\n13677:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:CL_0001063 \"https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/448\")\n13684:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:add\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:cg\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:wdd\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0001064 \"A neoplastic cell that is capable of entering a surrounding tissue\")\n13689:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:CL_0001064 \"https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/448\")\n13745:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:add\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:cvs\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:ymb\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:22796012\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:25851693\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0001070 \"An adipocyte that is beige in color, thermogenic, and which differentiates in white fat tissue from a Myf5-negative progenitor.\")\n14649:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"ISBN:0412046911\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:36138472\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:8540632\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"http://www.copewithcytokines.de\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002063 \"A pulmonary alveolar epithelial cell that modulates the fluid surrounding the alveolar epithelium by secreting and recycling surfactants via specialized organelles called alveolar lamellar bodies. This cell type also contributes to tissue repair and can differentiate after injury into a pulmonary alveolar type 1 cell. This cuboidal cell is thicker than squamous alveolar cells, has a rounded apical surface that projects above the level of the surrounding epithelium, and its free surface is covered by short microvilli.\")\n15023:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002094 \"A cell that makes up the loose connective tissue of the ovary.\")\n15465:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GO:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:1987287\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002135 \"Epidermal cells that do not contain keratin. Cell type is usually associated with moist epidermal tissues.\")\n15874:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:source <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/589>) obo:RO_0002161 obo:CL_0002174 obo:NCBITaxon_50557)\n16007:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"ISBN:0517223651\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:16824012\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:32187560\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"WIKIPEDIA:Basal_cell\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"doi:/10.1073/pnas.1734203100\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002187 \"A mitotically active, columnar-shaped keratinocyte located in the stratum basale of the epidermis and attached to the basement membrane. This cell exhibits heterogeneity, encompassing stem and progenitor subpopulations with distinct molecular and proliferative characteristics (Haensel et al., 2020), and serves as the primary source of new keratinocytes for epidermal renewal, homeostasis, and regeneration.\")\n16103:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso obo:CL_0002195 \"https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/800\")\n16109:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:17901986\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002196 \"A transient hepatic stem cell observed after liver injury with a high nuclear to cytoplasm ratio that can differentiate into mature hepatocytes and bile duct cells. Arises from more than one tissue.\")\n16665:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002247 \"A tissue macrophage that is in the pleural space.\")\n16748:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:11331626\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:30013421\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:30309298\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:31907034\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"Wikipedia:Decidualization\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41591-020-1040-z\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002255 \"A stromal cell of the endometrium, characterized by its fibroblast-like morphology, regenerative capacity, and ability to undergo decidualization. It differentiates into a decidual stromal cell during pregnancy, essential for embryo implantation and maintenance. This cell is involved in tissue proliferation, remodeling, and breakdown, responding to hormonal changes, particularly estrogen and progesterone.\")\n17112:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"ISBN:0517223651\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:15738192\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002287 \"A rounded, mitotically active stem cell which is the source of new cells of the taste bud; located basally.\")\n17194:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:9264335\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002294 \"An epithelial cell with a well defined Golgi apparatus that makes up the continuous layer of cells bordering the thymic tissue beneath the capsule.\")\n17495:# Class: obo:CL_0002320 (connective tissue cell)\n17497:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"ISBN:0618947256\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:19198652\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:26043871\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:31194383\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002320 \"A cell of the supporting or framework tissue of the body, which includes adipose tissue, cartilage, and bone. In vertebrates, this cell arises chiefly from the embryonic mesoderm and, in cranial regions, from neural crest.\")\n17503:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0002320 \"connective tissue cell\")\n17534:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"BFO:0000066\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:3211847\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002323 \"A cell of a fetus which is suspended in the amniotic fluid. Amniocytes are considered to arise from several tissues including fetal skin, the fetal urinary tract, umbilical cord, and the inner amniotic surface.\")\n17971:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:18180458\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002365 \"An epithelial cell of the medullary thymus. This cell type expresses a diverse range of tissue-specific antigens. This promiscuous gene expression is a cell-autonomous property of medullary epithelial cells and is maintained during the entire period of thymic T cell output.\")\n17992:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:14500801\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:40136401\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002367 \"An ocular connective tissue cell that is part of the eye's trabecular meshwork and is directly responsible for regulating the outflow of aqueous humor from the anterior chamber of the eye, thereby maintaining proper intraocular pressure.\")\n18035:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_0002371 <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/1150>)\n18839:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:hjd\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:15987436\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:16397499\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:17851544\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002451 \"A multi-fate stem cell that is the source of cells for growth of the mammary gland during puberty and gestation. This cell type gives rise to both the luminal and myoepithelial cell types of the gland, and have been shown to have the ability to regenerate the entire organ in mice. This cell type also plays an important role in carcinogenesis of the breast. This cell type is Lin-, CD24-positive, CD29-hi.\")\n18941:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"http://www.immgen.org/index_content.html\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002461 \"A conventional dendritic cell that is CD103-positive. This cell type is usually found in non-lymphoid tissue.\")\n18951:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"http://www.immgen.org/index_content.html\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002462 \"A F4/80-negative dendritic cell located in adipose tissue.\")\n19074:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"http://www.immgen.org/index_content.html\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002474 \"A MHC-II-negative classical monocyte located in lymphoid tissue that is F4/80-positive, CD11c-negative, and CD11b-high.\")\n19083:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"http://www.immgen.org/index_content.html\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002475 \"A MHC-II-negative classical monocyte located in lymphoid tissue that is F4/80-positive, CD11c-intermediate, and CD11b-high.\")\n19092:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"http://www.immgen.org/index_content.html\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002476 \"A tissue-resident macrophage located in the bone marrow. This cell type is B220-negative, CD3e-negative, Ly-6C-negative, CD115-positive, F4/80-positive.\")\n19104:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002477 \"A macrophage located in adipose tissue that is CD45-positive, CD11c-positive, and SIRPa-positive.\")\n19509:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:mh\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:12668625\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002519 \"An interrenal epithelial kidney cell is an epithelial cell found in the anterior kidney of teleosts fish. This cell type is arranged in layers around the posterior cardinal vein and contains many mitochondria with tubulovesicular cristae. Interrenal chromaffin cells are interspersed among the tissue layer created by this cell type.\")\n19527:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002521 \"An adipocyte that is part of subcutaneous adipose tissue.\")\n19557:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:19783135\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002524 \"A disseminated nephrocyte is a nephrocyte that filters hemolymph and is found at scattered locations in the fat body or other tissues.\")\n19776:# Class: obo:CL_0002548 (fibroblast of cardiac tissue)\n19782:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0002548 \"fibroblast of cardiac tissue\")\n19924:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002564 \"A connective tissue cell of the nucleus pulposus cell of intervertebral disc.\")\n19977:# Class: obo:CL_0002570 (mesenchymal stem cell of adipose tissue)\n19979:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002570 \"A mesenchymal stem cell of adipose tissue.\")\n19983:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0002570 \"mesenchymal stem cell of adipose tissue\")\n20059:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002578 \"A preadipocyte found in mesenteric tissue.\")\n20086:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:20106964\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:31079959\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002581 \"A preadipocyte that is part of a perirenal fat tissue.\")\n20095:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002582 \"A preadipocyte that is part of visceral tissue.\")\n20103:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002583 \"A preadipocyte that is part of subcutaneous tissue.\")\n20390:# Class: obo:CL_0002615 (adipocyte of omentum tissue)\n20392:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002615 \"An adipocyte that is part of omentum tissue.\")\n20397:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0002615 \"adipocyte of omentum tissue\")\n20403:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002616 \"An adipocyte of perirenal fat tissue.\")\n20420:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:CL_0002617 \"Adipocytes found in the subcutaneous adipose tissue of breast. The breast consists of three main components, the skin, the subcutaneous adipose tissue and the functional glandular tissue that comprises both parenchyma and stroma.[CALOHA]\")\n20443:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso obo:CL_0002619 \"https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/1410\")\n20444:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso obo:CL_0002619 \"https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/348\")\n20631:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:20942606\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002639 \"An amniotic stem cell is a mesenchymalstem cell extracted from amniotic fluid. Amniotic stem cells are able to differentiate into various tissue type such as skin, cartilage, cardiac tissue, nerves, muscle, and bone\")\n20882:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:18353863\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:37720106\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002665 \"A mesenchymal cell within the inner ear, specifically localised to supportive connective tissues such as the spiral ligament and spiral limbus. This cell has specialised structural and molecular adaptations, and it contributes to the homeostasis of the cochlear environment by participating in ionic regulation crucial for auditory function.\")\n20916:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:18353863\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:19277783\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:33193034\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002668 \"A spiral ligament fibrocyte that is located in the triangular space inferior to the crista basilaris (basilar crest). This cell is spindle-shaped and expresses NKCC1 but minimal to no Na,K-ATPase (ATP1A1, ATP1B1) in mice and humans, and lacks connexin 26/30 in mice, indicating it does not participate in K⁺ recycling, unlike type II fibrocytes. Type IV fibrocyte is uniquely characterised by strong expression of connective tissue growth factor (CTGF) in mice, suggesting roles in tissue remodelling and paracrine signalling to other cochlear cells.\")\n21031:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:20023630\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002679 \"A lymphocyte found in adipose tissue that lacks lineage markers of other lymphocytes but is capable of mediating TH2 cytokine responses. This cell type is found in fat associated lymphoid clusters, proliferates in response to IL2 and produce large amounts of TH2 cytokines such as IL5, IL6 and IL13\")\n22588:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso obo:CL_0005026 \"https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/800\")\n22605:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GO_REF:0000034\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0007001 \"Cell that has the potential to form a skeletal cell type (e.g. cells in periosteum, cells in marrow) and produce extracellular matrix (often mineralized) and skeletal tissue (often mineralized).\")\n22609:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:CL_0007001 \"Needs logical definition. Should be capable_of skeletal system morphogenesis? or skeletal tissue development? needs to be added to GO. NOTES:a cell type of the early embryo (see also: mesenchymal cells) that will give rise to mineralized connective tissue. Scleroblasts can differentiate into osteoblasts (bone-forming cells), chondroblasts (cartilage-forming cells), odontoblasts (dentin-forming cells), ameloblasts (enamel-forming cells). The mesenchymal cells developing into osteoblasts and chondroblasts are derived from the mesoderm. Those developing into odontoblasts are neural crest cells. Those developing into ameloblasts are derived from the ectoderm. (http://www.copewithcytokines.de/cope.cgi?key=scleroblasts)\")\n22683:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GO_REF:0000034\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0007009 \"Skeletogenic cell that has the potential to develop into a chondroblast; and arises from neural crest, meseosdermal and notochordal and connective tissue cells.\")\n22714:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GO_REF:0000034\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PSPUB:0000170\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0007012 \"Odontoblast that non-terminally differentiated, located in the odontogenic papilla and dentine tissue, and transforms from a odontoblast cell.\")\n22801:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/583\") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_0007021 \"club cell (zebrafish)\")\n22802:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"ISBN-10:0412429403\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/583\") oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:CL_0007021 \"club cell\")\n23021:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_0008029 <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/1540>)\n23030:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_0008030 <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/1540>)\n23175:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"DOI:10.1016/B978-0-443-10321-6.00004-7\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"Wikipedia:Extrafusal_muscle_fiber&oldid=978415293\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0008046 \"A skeletal muscle fiber that is innervated by alpha motor neuron and generates tension by contracting, thereby allowing for skeletal movement. These fibers make up the large mass of skeletal muscle tissue and are attached to bones by tendons.\")\n23222:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:26578857\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:35726097\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0008053 \"A capillary endothelial cell that is part of the circumventricular organs (CVOs), characterized by fenestrations that facilitate selective permeability to molecules, distinguishing it from the non-fenestrated endothelial cells of the blood-brain barrier. This cell is integral to the unique vascular structure of CVOs, which lack a traditional blood-brain barrier. It enables bidirectional exchange of polar molecules between blood and neural tissue, supporting neuroendocrine signaling, fluid balance, and immune responses. It is marked by the expression of PLVAP, a component of the fenestral diaphragm, in both rodents and humans.\")\n23292:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso obo:CL_0009003 \"https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/710\")\n23733:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:26551552\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4183-8865\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0009044 \"A lymphocyte that resides in the lamina propria of the small intestine. Lamina propria leukocytes and intraepithelial lymphocytes are the effector compartments of the gut mucosal immune system. Lymphocytes circulate through gut associated lymphoid tissues until recruitment by intestinal antigens. They are involved in the gut immune response.\")\n24297:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:20081841\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:22045730\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:24605102\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0009099 \"A progenitor cell that is a tissue-resident mesenchymal cell, important for skeletal muscle regeneration, and able to differentiate into both adipocytes or fibroblasts.\")\n24316:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0009101 \"A reticular cell involved in directing B cells and T cells to specific regions within a tissue.\")\n24321:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso obo:CL_0009101 \"https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/1472\")\n24333:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso obo:CL_0009102 \"https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/1472\")\n24344:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso obo:CL_0009103 \"https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/1472\")\n24358:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso obo:CL_0009104 \"https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/1472\")\n24371:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso obo:CL_0009105 \"https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/1472\")\n24386:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso obo:CL_0009106 \"https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/1472\")\n24459:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:15578096\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:26501424\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:29769249\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://orcid.org/0000-0001-7655-4833\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0009113 \"A regulatory T cell present in the B cell follicles and germinal centers of lymphoid tissues. In humans, it is CXCR5+.\")\n24484:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso obo:CL_0009115 \"https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/1472\")\n24499:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/417>) Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:2478566\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:28445731\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:30420661\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0010000 \"A differentiating matrix keratinocyte that has initiated terminal differentiation and keratin synthesis. This cell undergoes a characteristic change while migrating upward from the hair follicle bulb, representing a committed progenitor cell destined to form the hair medulla, cortex and hair root sheath.\")\n24524:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_0010003 <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/2429>)\n24568:SubClassOf(Annotation(rdfs:seeAlso \"https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/2245\") obo:CL_0010009 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000050 obo:UBERON_0000966))\n24596:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso obo:CL_0010012 \"https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/1359\")\n24630:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso obo:CL_0010017 <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/786>)\n24792:# Class: obo:CL_0011018 (lymphoid tissue–inducer cell)\n24794:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:add\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:19029905\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:21601793\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:27935637\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:29454785\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:9354470\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0011018 \"A group 3 innate lymphoid cell that express ROR gamma t and IL-7R alpha in the absence of lineage markers (e.g. CD3, CD19, B220, CD11c, Gr-1), with the functional ability to interact with mesenchymal cells through lymphotoxin and tumor necrosis factor. Lymphoid tissue-inducer cells are key to the development of lymph nodes and Peyer’s patches.\")\n24797:AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_0011018 \"lymphoid tissue inducer cell\")\n24798:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0011018 \"lymphoid tissue–inducer cell\")\n24873:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:28369879\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://orcid.org/0000-0001-6164-0667\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0011027 \"Any fibroblast that is part of skeletal muscle tissue.\")\n25088:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:30069044\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:30069046\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0017000 \"An ionocyte that is part of the lung epithelium. The cells from this type are major sources of the CFTR protein in human and mice.\")\n25195:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_0017501 <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/734>)\n25232:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_0017505 <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/2251>)\n25416:# Class: obo:CL_0020000 (gut mesenchymal lymphoid tissue organizer cell)\n25418:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:31324739\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:33707780\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:34046914\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:34497389\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0020000 \"A mesenchymal stromal cell found in the gut during development, and interacts with lymphoid tissue inducer (LTi) cells to drive secondary lymphoid organ formation (Prados et al., 2021). It expresses LTβR, PDGFRα, and VCAM, and upon activation secretes chemokines(e.g. CXCL13) and cytokines to organise lymphoid tissue architecture and recruit immune cells in both mice and humans (Denton el al., 2019; Elmentaite et al., 2021). As a multipotent progenitor, an mLTo cell differentiates into adult stromal subsets, including fibroblastic reticular cells (FRCs) (Prados et al., 2021), and its descendants persist in the adult gut-associated lymphoid tissues to maintain immune homeostasis(Denton el al., 2019; Lütge et al., 2021).\")\n25422:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0020000 \"gut mesenchymal lymphoid tissue organizer cell\")\n25438:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:26382249\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:33352411\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:35641678\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:40781543\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0020002 \"A mucosal-associated invariant T (MAIT) cell found in various tissues including human blood, liver, gut, and lung, characterised by a semi-invariant T cell receptor (TCR) comprising TRAV1-2 (Vα7.2) predominantly paired with TRAJ33, and high expression of CD161. Upon activation, MAIT cells produce inflammatory cytokines, including IFN-γ, TNF-α, and IL-17.\")\n25451:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:11596046\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:28384468\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:28949036\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:35927027\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:7127069\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0020003 \"A projection neuron that has its soma located in the internal segment of the globus pallidus (GPi) of primates, in the central “core” region. It expresses parvalbumin, is primarily GABAergic, and projects to motor thalamic nuclei including the ventral anterior, ventrolateral, and ventromedial nuclei. In the rodent homolog (entopeduncular nucleus), the core subregion is characterized by concentrated parvalbumin-positive neurons targeting the ventral anterior-ventral lateral thalamic nucleus and receives inputs from the dorsolateral striatum innervated by sensorimotor cortices (Miyamoto & Fukuda, 2022). This neuron corresponds to the sensorimotor output population of the GPi, as demonstrated in human tissue and cross-species comparative studies (Wallace et al., 2017) and by classic tracing in primates (Parent & De Bellefeuille, 1982; Parent et al., 2001).\")\n25507:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:10377365\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:10934256\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:21925242\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:2329464\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:25374536\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:25876458\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:29651049\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:37905876\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0020008 \"A large aspiny interneuron of the striatum that serves as the principal intrinsic source of acetylcholine while simultaneously releasing GABA. It is defined by coexpression of cholinergic markers (ChAT and VAChT/Slc18a3) and GABAergic markers (GAD65/Gad2 and VGAT/Slc32a1) (Lozovaya et al., 2018). This MGE-derived neuron maintains Lhx6 expression associated with dual-transmitter identity (Fragkouli et al., 2009; Marin et al., 2000). Morphologically, it possesses a large soma (20–50 μm diameter), 2–4 thick aspiny dendrites extending radially ~1 mm, and locally ramified axons with hundreds of thousands of varicosities (Wilson et al., 1990; Lim et al., 2014).\")\n25560:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:31857493\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:35069694\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0020013 \"A fibroblast forming the connective tissue sheath that encloses the hair follicle epithelium from the level of the bulge downward to the hair bulb. This cell expresses characteristic markers, including DPEP1 in humans and COL11A1 and ACTA2 (alpha-smooth muscle actin) in mice and humans. Dermal sheath fibroblast possesses contractile properties mediated by calcium-calmodulin-myosin light chain kinase signalling, generating centripetal force during hair follicle catagen that relocates the dermal papilla to the stem cell niche.\")\n25574:AnnotationAssertion(dc:creator obo:CL_0020014 \"GitHub Copilot\")\n25588:AnnotationAssertion(dc:creator obo:CL_0020015 \"GitHub Copilot\")\n25599:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:34280086\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"doi:10.1101/2023.12.07.570646\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0020016 \"A tissue-specific stem cell with long-term self-renewal capacity that is restricted to generating bone, cartilage, and stromal cell lineages. This cell is primarily located in the growth plate (resting zone), periosteum, and subchondral bone regions, and is marked by high clonogenicity and robust multipotent differentiation potential in both osteogenic and chondrogenic lineages. Distinctively, ocSSC lacks adipogenic capacity, distinguishing it from other mesenchymal stem cell populations.\")\n25601:AnnotationAssertion(terms:creator obo:CL_0020016 \"GitHub Copilot\")\n25614:AnnotationAssertion(dc:creator obo:CL_0020017 \"GitHub Copilot\")\n25625:AnnotationAssertion(terms:creator obo:CL_0020018 \"GitHub Copilot\")\n25638:AnnotationAssertion(dc:creator obo:CL_0020019 \"GitHub Copilot\")\n25646:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:30026257\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:34045450\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:34400611\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:37660146\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0020020 \"A chondrocyte identified in both healthy and osteoarthritic human articular cartilage, and in intervertebral disc tissue. In healthy articular cartilage, a homeostatic chondrocyte is characterised by the expression of TXNIP, IFITM3, GDF15, and TIMP1 (Wang et al., 2021), among other extracellular matrix and regulatory molecules. This cell maintains cartilage integrity by producing key ECM components and supporting baseline tissue homeostasis. In OA cartilage, this cell is enriched for genes related to cellular homeostasis modulation, including regulation of the cell cycle, development, and RNA metabolism (Ji et al., 2018).\")\n25647:AnnotationAssertion(dc:creator obo:CL_0020020 \"GitHub Copilot\")\n25657:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:31871141\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:36338137\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0020021 \"A mesenchymal progenitor cell located in fibrocartilaginous tissues, along the fibrochondrocytic differentiation pathway that co-expresses both fibrochondrocyte markers (COL1A1, COL3A1) and mesenchymal stem cell markers (MCAM/CD146, MYLK) in humans. This cell serves as a progenitor for mature fibrochondrocytes and other meniscal cell types, with differentiation regulated by TGF-β signaling, focal adhesion, and extracellular matrix-receptor interaction pathways.\")\n25659:AnnotationAssertion(terms:creator obo:CL_0020021 \"GitHub Copilot\")\n25669:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:29985449\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:31871141\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:34137454\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0020022 \"A post-proliferative chondrocyte in the prehypertrophic zone of the cartilage tissue, located between the proliferative and hypertrophic zones. This cell is characterised by increased cell volume and expression of Indian Hedgehog (Ihh), PTH1R, and Runx2/3 in both humans and mice (Hallett et al., 2021). It coordinates the PTHrP-Ihh feedback loop that regulates chondrocyte differentiation and functions as a signalling hub for communication between proliferative chondrocytes, hypertrophic chondrocytes, and periosteal osteoblasts (Hallett et al., 2021).\")\n25678:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:21652695\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:30026257\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:34400611\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:38868774\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:40753276\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0020023 \"A metabolically highly active chondrocyte that is identified in the cartilaginous tissues, including articular cartilage and intervertebral disc cartilage. In the human articular cartilage, this cell is marked by genes such as CYTL1, FRZB, CLEC3A, and S100B (Ji et al., 2018; Raut et al.,2025). This cell demonstrates strong metabolic activity and is implicated in extracellular matrix regulation and adaptive responses (Ji et al., 2018; Gan et al., 2021; Raut et al.,2025).\")\n25680:AnnotationAssertion(terms:creator obo:CL_0020023 \"GitHub Copilot\")\n25691:AnnotationAssertion(terms:creator obo:CL_0020024 \"GitHub Copilot\")\n25706:AnnotationAssertion(terms:creator obo:CL_0020025 \"GitHub Copilot\")\n25719:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_0020026 \"https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/3493\")\n25721:AnnotationAssertion(terms:creator obo:CL_0020026 \"GitHub Copilot\")\n25735:AnnotationAssertion(terms:creator obo:CL_0020027 \"GitHub Copilot\")\n26109:# Class: obo:CL_1000299 (fibroblast of connective tissue of prostate)\n26111:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_1000299 \"A fibroblast that is part of the connective tissue of prostate.\")\n26113:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_1000299 \"fibroblast of connective tissue of prostate\")\n26125:# Class: obo:CL_1000301 (fibroblast of subepithelial connective tissue of prostatic gland)\n26127:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_1000301 \"A fibroblast that is part of the subepithelial connective tissue of prostatic gland.\")\n26129:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_1000301 \"fibroblast of subepithelial connective tissue of prostatic gland\")\n26141:# Class: obo:CL_1000303 (fibroblast of areolar connective tissue)\n26143:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_1000303 \"A fibroblast that is part of the areolar connective tissue.\")\n26145:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_1000303 \"fibroblast of areolar connective tissue\")\n26150:# Class: obo:CL_1000304 (fibroblast of connective tissue of nonglandular part of prostate)\n26152:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_1000304 \"A fibroblast that is part of the connective tissue of nonglandular part of prostate.\")\n26154:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_1000304 \"fibroblast of connective tissue of nonglandular part of prostate\")\n26158:# Class: obo:CL_1000305 (fibroblast of connective tissue of glandular part of prostate)\n26160:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_1000305 \"A fibroblast that is part of the connective tissue of glandular part of prostate.\")\n26162:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_1000305 \"fibroblast of connective tissue of glandular part of prostate\")\n26174:# Class: obo:CL_1000307 (fibroblast of dense regular elastic tissue)\n26176:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_1000307 \"A fibroblast that is part of the dense regular elastic tissue.\")\n26178:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_1000307 \"fibroblast of dense regular elastic tissue\")\n27880:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_1000698 \"A tissue-resident macrophage that is part of some kidney.\")\n28220:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:CL_1001064 \"Note that the source of this term is KUPO:0001096 (artery smooth muscle cell), which we believe actually denotes the kidney specific structure.\")\n28228:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:CL_1001066 \"Note that the source of this term is KUPO:0001098 (arteriole smooth muscle cell), which we believe actually denotes the kidney specific structure.\")\n28236:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:CL_1001068 \"Note that the source of this term is KUPO:0001068 (venous system smooth muscle cell), which we believe actually denotes the kidney specific structure.\")\n28695:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_1001566 <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/1443>)\n28842:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"HPA:Breast\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_1001583 \"OBSOLETE: Adipocytes found in the subcutaneous adipose tissue of breast. The breast consists of three main components, the skin, the subcutaneous adipose tissue and the functional glandular tissue that comprises both parenchyma and stroma.\")\n29018:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"HPA:HPA\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"NPX:PDR\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_1001603 \"Circulating macrophages and tissue macrophages (alveolar macrophages) of lung.\")\n29101:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"BTO:0002770\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"NCIT:C32429\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:30013421\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:30429548\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"Wiki:Decidual_cells&oldid=937509751\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"Wiki:Decidualization&oldid=908981933\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_2000002 \"A specialized, enlarged, connective tissue cell of the decidua with enlarged nucleus, dense membrane‐bound secretory granules and cytoplasmic accumulation of glycogen and lipid droplets. These cells develop by the transformation of endometrial stromal cells during decidualization.\")\n29693:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:TermGenie\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:12052228\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:15749958\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:23467088\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:31672973\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:36205477\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_2000064 \"A meso-epithelial cell of the ovarian surface epithelium, varying in shape from flat to cuboidal to pseudostratified columnar. This cell plays a crucial role in ovulation by producing proteolytic enzymes in response to gonadotropins, facilitating follicle rupture through tissue breakdown and TNF-alpha signaling. Additionally, this cell contributes to post-ovulatory wound repair (Morris et al., 2022) and exhibits stem cell-like properties (Flesken-Nikitin et al., 2013; Wang et al., 2019).\")\n29772:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:TermGenie\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_2000072 \"Any microvascular endothelial cell that is part of a adipose tissue.\")\n29818:# Class: obo:CL_2000077 (skeletal muscle tissue of pectoralis major striated muscle cell)\n29820:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:TermGenie\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_2000077 \"Any striated muscle cell that is part of a skeletal muscle tissue of pectoralis major.\")\n29824:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_2000077 \"skeletal muscle tissue of pectoralis major striated muscle cell\")\n29845:# Class: obo:CL_2000080 (mesenchymal stem cell of abdominal adipose tissue)\n29847:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:TermGenie\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_2000080 \"Any mesenchymal stem cell of adipose tissue that is part of an abdomen.\")\n29852:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_2000080 \"mesenchymal stem cell of abdominal adipose tissue\")\n29867:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_2000082 <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/1562>)\n30030:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:CellBLAST\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:30429548\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:30498493\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:34745147\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:35438172\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_3000001 \"A tissue-resident macrophage that is part of the placenta. A Hofbauer cell expresses high levels of growth factors and metalloproteinases that support vasculogenesis, angiogenesis, branching morphogenesis and tissue remodeling. A Hofbauer cell has a fetal origin, is found in the villous stroma, chorion, and amnion, and is present throughout pregnancy.\")\n30489:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_4023034 <https://github.com/obophenotype/brain_data_standards_ontologies/issues/231>)\n30842:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_4023067 <https://github.com/obophenotype/brain_data_standards_ontologies/issues/231>)\n30879:in the label. See comments in issue 2357.\")\n30907:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_4023073 <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/2043>)\n30958:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_4023078 <https://github.com/obophenotype/brain_data_standards_ontologies/issues/231>)\n31643:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"doi:10.1016/bs.ctdb.2020.10.008\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4029003 \"A gamete-nursing cell that derives from the somatic tissues of the gonad (del Pino, 2021).\")\n31682:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"doi:10.1038/s41467-020-20082-7\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4030003 \"A cell that makes up the loose connective tissue of the thymus.\")\n31712:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ccr.2013.10.013\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://www.proteinatlas.org/learn/dictionary/normal/fallopian+tube\") rdfs:comment obo:CL_4030006 \"The other cell types of the fallopian tube epithelium include the ciliated cell and the intercalary cell, or peg cell. The fallopian tube secretory epithelial cell type has been implicated as a source of high-grade serous ovarian carcinoma.\")\n31720:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:16565155\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://www.proteinatlas.org/humanproteome/tissue/fallopian+tube\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4030007 \"A multi-ciliated epithelial cell that is part of the fallopian tube, mainly found on the apex of the mucosal folds. This cell exhibits a columnar shape with an oval nucleus and is characterized by the presence of cilia on its surface. The coordinated beating of these cilia, together with peristaltic contractions, contributes to the self-propulsion of spermatozoa, the transport of ovum during ovulation and the transport of the fertilized ovum to the intramural fallopian tube.\")\n31889:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:16230044\") rdfs:comment obo:CL_4030025 \"Renal cortical fibroblasts have key roles in mediating intercellular communication with neighboring/infiltrating cells and extracellular matrix and maintenance of renal tissue architecture. They additionally have an endocrine function in the production of epoetin.\")\n31936:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:24987007\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4030031 \"Any cell that is located within the interstitium between the cells most prominent in defining a given tissue. \\\"Interstitial cell\\\" is a morphological term and refers to a variety of cells with differing origins and phenotypes.\")\n31943:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref <https://orcid.org/0000-0001-7655-4833>) Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:25311230\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://doi.org/10.1098/rsif.2017.0580\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://doi.org/10.1161/ATVBAHA.120.314789\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://doi.org/10.1161/JAHA.117.006339\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://doi.org/10.2353/ajpath.2007.070251\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4030032 \"An interstitial cell that is part of a cardiac valve leaflet. Along with valve endothelial cells, a valve interstitial cell maintains tissue homeostasis for the function of cardiac valves through secreting biochemical signals, matrix proteins and matrix remodeling enzymes.\")\n31955:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:25311230\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4030033 \"An endothelial cell that lines a surface of a cardiac valve leaflet. Along with valve interstitial cells, a valve endothelial cell maintains tissue homeostasis for the function of cardiac valves through secreting biochemical signals, matrix proteins and matrix remodeling enzymes.\")\n31959:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:25311230\") rdfs:comment obo:CL_4030033 \"At least in some mammalian species, a valve endothelial cell expresses CD31. Valve endothelial cells can undergo an endothelial to mesenchymal transition to acquire a fibroblast or myofibroblast phenotype that leads to changes in the microenvironmental signals and facilitates tissue regeneration.\")\n32510:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:33351116\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4033018 \"A megakaryocyte that is resident in the lung connective tissue.\")\n32769:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:33382972\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:35017701\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:36211428\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4033043 \"A macrophage that is part of the lung connective tissue (pulmonary interstitium). This cell performs tissue remodeling and contributes to barrier immunity through antigen presentation.\")\n32895:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:24928499\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:27889329\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4033054 \"A cell that is adjacent to a vessel. A perivascular cell plays a crucial role in maintaining vascular function and tissue homeostasis. This cell type regulates vessel integrity and flow dynamics.\")\n33022:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:36113384\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4033064 \"A tissue-resident macrophage that is part of the uterus.\")\n33114:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:31257031\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:34876704\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:35712121\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4033086 \"A tissue-resident macrophage that is associated with lipids. This cell responsible of anti-inflammatory functions, lipid accumulation and enhancing phagocytosis (Wculek et al., 2022, Liu et al., 2022). In mice and humans, a lipid-associated macrophage is characterized by the marker Trem2 (Jaitlin et al., 2019).\")\n33119:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:35021063\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:37153549\") rdfs:comment obo:CL_4033086 \"Distinct from “adipose macrophage” in Trem2 expression, and is not limited to adipose tissue. Guilliams et al. describe these cells as being distinct from Kupffer cells.\")\n33126:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:39007150\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4033087 \"A tissue-resident macrophage that is part of the placenta. This cell helps preventing immunological rejection of the fetus by modulating the immune environment. A placental resident macrophage has high plasticity to adapt to the changing needs of each phase of pregnancy.\")\n33313:# Class: obo:CL_4040004 (mesenchymal stem cell of orbital adipose tissue)\n33315:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref <https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0819-0473>) obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4040004 \"Any mesenchymal stem cell of adipose tissue that is part of an orbital region.\")\n33321:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_4040004 \"mesenchymal stem cell of orbital adipose tissue\")\n33914:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:17893694\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:35406678\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4047029 \"An endothelial cell forming the walls of capillaries immediately downstream from arterioles, facilitating the exchange of substances between blood and interstitial fluid. This cell is characterized by a small diameter and may be continuous, fenestrated, or sinusoidal, depending on their location and function. This cell plays a crucial role in tissue oxygenation, nutrient delivery, and maintaining homeostasis within the microvascular network.\")\n33922:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"DOI:10.1038/s41467-022-33324-7\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:23620236\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4047030 \"A specialized endothelial cell located in the transition zone between capillaries and small venules in the microvascular system. It exhibits characteristics of both capillary and venous cells, expressing markers from both types. This cell plays a role in regulating blood flow and substance exchange between blood and tissues.\")\n33958:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:11146428\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:32424507\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4047036 \"A smooth muscle cell located in the middle layer of the muscularis externa of the stomach wall. This cell is arranged concentrically with the stomach's longitudinal axis, forming a continuous sheet of contractile tissue. It is fusiform in shape, containing actin and myosin filaments that enable contraction without striations. This cell contributes to the mechanical digestion and movement of food within the stomach through coordinated contractions, and plays a role in forming the pyloric sphincter in the pyloric region.\")\n34003:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"DOI:10.3389/fncel.2023.1170309\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:28487635\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:33731961\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4047043 \"An enteric glial cell that is associated with neuron cell bodies within submucosal ganglia of the gastrointestinal tract. This cell is located in the submucosa, a layer of connective tissue beneath the intestinal mucosa. This cell plays a role in modulating secretomotor neuron activity and contributes to the regulation of digestive fluid secretion and absorption.\")\n34071:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:33033253\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:33628208\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:35746551\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:36750615\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:39369473\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4047053 \"A macrophage characterized by high expression of Triggering Receptor Expressed on Myeloid cells 2 (TREM2), found in various tissues including the liver, adipose tissue, bone, gut (Colonna, 2023), and tumor microenvironments, where it is associated with immunosuppressive and anti-inflammatory activity (Colmenares, 2024; Khantakova, 2022). This cell exhibits a distinct gene expression profile in the tumor microenvironment, including overexpression of complement system genes (C1QA, C1QB, C1QC, C3), and SPP1 in both mice and humans (Xiong, 2020; Khantakova, 2022). It is involved in phagocytosis, tissue repair, and modulation of immune responses (Coeho, 2021).\")\n34081:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:28845904\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:33681216\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:34302064\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4047054 \"A dendritic cell capable of capturing antigens in peripheral tissues and migrating through blood or lymphatic vessels to secondary lymphoid organs, where it presents processed antigens to T cells. It expresses MHC class II molecules and lacks lineage markers associated with other leukocyte populations, distinguishing it as a member of the dendritic cell lineage. This cell plays a pivotal role in initiating and regulating adaptive immune responses by linking peripheral antigen capture with T cell activation in lymphoid tissues.\")\n34122:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:34230995\") rdfs:comment obo:CL_4047101 \"Liver-resident natural killer cells play a crucial role in modulating the immune response during inflammation by balancing cytotoxic activity and cytokine production, thereby contributing to the maintenance of liver homeostasis and protection against excessive tissue damage.\")\n34160:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:33707780\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:35440118\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4052004 \"A specialized fibroblastic reticular cell that is part of gut-associated lymphoid tissue (GALT), responsible for forming a structural network that facilitates immune cell interactions. This cell is found in Peyer's patches, cryptopatches, and isolated lymphoid follicles. It plays crucial roles in maintaining intestinal immunity by controlling innate lymphoid cell homeostasis and function, organizing lymphoid structures, and contributing to intestinal microbiome balance.\")\n34165:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:35440118\") oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym obo:CL_4052004 \"solitary intestinal lymphoid tissue fibroblastic reticular cell\")\n34245:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:29339448\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:34634036\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:7107724\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4052011 \"An enterocyte found in the follicle-associated epithelium (FAE) that covers Peyer's patches and other mucosa-associated lymphoid tissues. This cell has reduced absorptive capacity and expresses higher levels of chemokines CCL20 and CXCL16, compared to regular villus enterocytes. It contributes to antigen sampling and immune interactions, supporting the specialized function of the FAE in mucosal immunity.\")\n34343:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"doi:/10.1101/2022.06.17.496207\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4052020 \"A granzyme K-associated CD8 T cell that resides in the lung, characterized by the expression of granzyme K (GZMK), and tissue residency markers CD103 and CD49a. This cell exhibits cytotoxic potential through its expression of multiple granzymes (GZMA, GZMB, GZMH, GZMM) in addition to GZMK.\")\n34354:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:35704599\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:37449888\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4052021 \"A CD8 T cell characterized by high expression of Granzyme K (GZMK). This cell is enriched in both inflamed tissues, such as synovial tissue in rheumatoid arthritis and respiratory tissues during COVID-19, as well as non-inflamed tissues like the gut and kidneys. Unlike highly cytotoxic GZMB+ CD8+ T cell, GZMK+ CD8+ T cell exhibits lower direct cytotoxic potential, and is involved in producing pro-inflammatory cytokines such as IFN-γ and TNF-α.\")\n34358:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:35704599\") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_4052021 \"tissue-enriched expressing GzmK CD8 cell\")\n34411:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:34727990\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:35908794\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:36361732\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"doi:10.1101/2024.05.15.594276\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4052026 \"A fast type II muscle cell that is part of the skeletal muscle tissue. This cell is characterized by its intermediate metabolic profile, utilizing both glycolytic and oxidative pathways for energy production. In humans, it is distinguished by the expression of myosin heavy chain 1 (MYH1).\")\n34445:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:36543915\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:36769178\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:37291214\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4052029 \"A fibroblast that is located beneath the visceral pleura of the lung. This cell contributes to the development of idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis (IPF) by forming fibrotic lesions that originate subpleurally and extend into lung tissue through activation, proliferation, migration, and differentiation into a myofibroblast.\")\n34489:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:35245089\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:37863104\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4052033 \"A tuft cell that is part of the extrahepatic biliary tree, including the gallbladder and extrahepatic bile ducts. It shares a core genetic program with intestinal tuft cells but has unique tissue-specific genes. Biliary tuft cell functions as a bile acid-sensitive regulator, suppressing inflammation and modulating microbiome-dependent neutrophil infiltration in biliary tissues. Unlike its intestinal counterparts, this cell decreases in number postnatally as bile acid production matures, with its abundance negatively regulated by bile acids.\")\n34545:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:30379593\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:34932383\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:38306414\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4052038 \"A tuft cell that is part of the nasal cavity respiratory epithelium. Acting as a chemosensor, it detects bitter taste ligands and bacterial signals via taste receptors, maintaining epithelial-microbial homeostasis by stimulating antimicrobial peptide secretion from adjacent epithelial cells. This cell is a major source of IL-25, promoting type 2 immune responses and potentially contributing to chronic rhinosinusitis (O'Leary et al., 2019).\")\n34573:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:30379593\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:32733896\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4052041 \"A tuft cell that is part of the epithelium of the pharyngotympanic (auditory) tube. This chemosensory cell is often positioned near cholinoreceptive sensory nerve fibers, suggesting a role in neuroimmune communication. It detects chemical signals and releases neuropeptides, such as acetylcholine (ACh) and CGRP, which contribute to inflammatory responses that help protect deeper tissues from harmful substances.\")\n34821:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:21049082\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:35701396\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:40538750\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"Wikipedia:Fibroblast\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"doi:/10.1038/s41427-020-0226-7\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4052071 \"A fibroblast in a quiescent state, characterized by a smaller, spindle-shaped morphology with a relatively small cytoplasm, modest rough endoplasmic reticulum and condensed chromatin. Despite low proliferation and contractility, it maintains high metabolic activity for extracellular-matrix homeostasis through continuous matrix protein turnover and mechanosensitive signaling. This cell can rapidly differentiate into contractile myofibroblasts under injury or inflammatory cues to drive tissue repair.\")\n34825:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID: 35701396\") rdfs:comment obo:CL_4052071 \"Historically, quiescent fibroblasts in uninjured tissues were often called “fibrocytes”, but this distinction faded over time, and the term “fibroblast” came to be used universally, regardless of activation state. Fibrocyte has been repurposed to describe a distinct population of bone marrow–derived, circulating cells that home to sites of injury and contribute to tissue repair and fibrosis.\")\n35069:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:26813785\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:31561945\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:37740916\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4072015 \"A Kupffer cell located within the hepatic sinusoids, derived from circulating bone marrow monocytes that acquire the phenotypic markers and functional properties of resident Kupffer cells through niche-dependent imprinting, typically following depletion of the embryonically derived Kupffer cell population. During liver injury, MoKCs become the dominant Kupffer cell subset, exhibiting enhanced proliferation, resistance to apoptosis, and key roles in tissue repair and fibrosis attenuation.\")\n35454:# Class: obo:CL_7770002 (juxtacanalicular tissue cell)\n35456:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:25356439\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:32341164\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:39829808\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_7770002 \"A trabecular meshwork cell of the juxtacanalicular tissue (JCT), characterized by a spindle-shaped, fibroblast-like morphology within a loose extracellular matrix immediately adjacent to Schlemm's canal. It expresses CHI3L1 (human and mouse) and ANGPTL7 (human), as well as smooth muscle actin for contractility. Unlike other trabecular meshwork cells, it does not form monolayers but exists in a loose network, regulating aqueous humor outflow resistance through continuous ECM remodelling, mechanotransduction, and formation of intracellular pores historically called giant vacuoles.\")\n35457:AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000233 obo:CL_7770002 \"https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/3275\")\n35461:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_7770002 \"juxtacanalicular tissue cell\")\n35477:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:17020902\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:30837987\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:32187560\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:32726565\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:37672481\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_7770004 \"An epithelial cell that resides in the layer(s) immediately above the basal layer in stratified or pseudostratified epithelia. This cell mainly originates from basal cells and is found across various tissues, including skin, esophagus, oral mucosa, airway, and cornea.\")\n35485:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:25356439\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:27443500\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_7770005 \"A trabecular meshwork cell that is part of the eye's trabecular meshwork, residing in the uveal and corneoscleral meshwork regions and serving as the primary biological filter in the aqueous humor drainage cascade. This cell exhibits endothelial-like properties, including production of antithrombogenic substances such as tissue plasminogen activator, while also demonstrating phagocytic activity to remove cellular debris from aqueous humour before the fluid moves deeper into the less porous juxtacanalicular tissue.\")\n35496:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:25356439\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:32341164\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:39829808\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_7770006 \"A trabecular meshwork cell within the eye's trabecular meshwork. This cell is molecularly distinguished by TMEFF2 expression in humans. A beam B cell is spatially intermingled with beam A cells throughout the uveal and corneoscleral meshwork regions, but demonstrates a tendency to localise closer to the juxtacanalicular tissue.\")\n35583:SubClassOf(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref <https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/757>) ObjectIntersectionOf(obo:CL_0000540 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0002162 obo:NCBITaxon_7742)) ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0002202 obo:CL_0000031))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
